From 8d07301a5b4fa6df4c93838ac683e45c2ca3e28a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bram Cohen <bram@chia.net>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 17:31:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Break exact ties in Boolean3 with one consistent perturbation

Exact ties between P and Q were each broken by their own rule: vertex
normals for x, the edge's face normals for y, face normals for z. Those
rules don't describe one perturbed configuration, and where the
tiebreak itself came out exactly zero (common with axis-aligned or
grid-aligned input) every predicate silently answered "no". Unions of
convex pieces with exactly coplanar faces, as in a Minkowski sum, could
then come out with a zero-volume flap of inverted triangles depending
on the order of the operands.

Now every tie is decided as it would come out in a single perturbed
configuration: each vertex moves along a displacement (offsetting all
of its face planes alike where it has at most three, so flat faces stay
flat and coincident faces stay tied), P moves once more by a smaller
amount so that it is the outer one where faces coincide, and then P is
translated along +x, +y and +z. A tie is the sign of a 2D cross product
or a 3x3 determinant of vertex differences, evaluated term by term in
order of significance; terms within rounding error are taken as zero.

Ties in degenerate projections (an edge perpendicular to x, edges
parallel in projection, a face parallel to z) keep the earlier rules.

Adds Boolean.CoplanarUnionOrder. Boolean.Simplify's RemoveDegenerates
count changes from 36 to 34 triangles; both are the same valid box.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
